not only the state action pairs but also
the

234
00:14:25,429 --> 00:14:30,070
intermediate steps or computations leading
to those actions. For

235
00:14:30,070 --> 00:14:33,230
instance, in a maze navigation task, the
expert might

236
00:14:33,230 --> 00:14:36,070
use a search algorithm to find the optimal
path,

237
00:14:36,070 --> 00:14:38,990
and the intermediate steps of this search
process are

238
00:14:38,990 --> 00:14:43,149
recorded alongside the final action.
During training, the model

239
00:14:43,149 --> 00:14:46,190
learns to predict the sequence of
intermediate steps leading

240
00:14:46,190 --> 00:14:49,029
to the final action using a sequential
model such

241
00:14:49,029 --> 00:14:52,590
as a transformer capable of handling the
autoregressive nature

242
00:14:52,590 --> 00:14:55,950
of the task. The model maximizes the
likelihood of

243
00:14:55,950 --> 00:15:00,110
the joint distribution of procedure
observations and expert actions.

244
00:15:00,669 --> 00:15:04,029
During inference. The model generates a
sequence of intermediate

245
00:15:04,029 --> 00:15:07,350
steps based on the input state, mimicking
the expert's

246
00:15:07,350 --> 00:15:11,360
procedure before outputting the final
action. This method allows

247
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:14,240
the model to replicate the expert's
decision making process

248
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:18,000
more accurately even in new and unseen
environments. At

249
